Issue confirmed.
Session is lost in case it has been destroyed and then reopened.
This happens because after being reopened session rerturns new session cookie value to the browser, while `session_id()` value remains to be the same. Check following 'logout' code:

``` php
public function actionLogout()
    {
        var_dump($_COOKIE); // session coookie value = sess_id#1

        var_dump(Yii::$app->getSession()->getId()); // outputs: sess_id#1

        Yii::$app->user->logout();

        Yii::$app->session->setFlash('success', 'You have been successfully logged out!');
        Yii::$app->session->set('logout', __METHOD__ . ' at ' . date('Y-m-d H:i:s'));

        var_dump(Yii::$app->getSession()->getId()); // outputs: sess_id#1 !!!

        exit;
    }
```

While network console indicates response from 'POST logout' containing 'Set-Cookie: PHPSESSID=sess_id#2' !!!

I am unsure why this happens.

The walk-around could be the regenerating session ID before return logout response:

```
public function actionLogout()
    {
        Yii::$app->user->logout();

        Yii::$app->session->setFlash('success', 'You have been successfully logged out!');
        Yii::$app->session->set('logout', __METHOD__ . ' at ' . date('Y-m-d H:i:s'));

        Yii::$app->session->regenerateID(); // Enforce new session ID usage

        return $this->goHome();
    }
```

In this case 2 'Set-Cookie' headers are passed to response and `session_id()` value updated.

It seems that new session ID cookie is sent once `session_destory()` is invoked.

The possible fix can be setting session ID to `null` manually at `Session::destroy()`:

``` php
public function destroy()
    {
        if ($this->getIsActive()) {
            @session_unset();
            @session_destroy();
            @session_id(null); // unset cached session ID
        }
    }
```

This works for me, but I am unsure if this is a correct way.
